---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
const title = "Mean, Median and Mode: Which Average Should You Use? - Toolkit Lab";
const description = "The difference between mean, median and mode, when each is the better summary of a list of numbers, and how outliers change the result.";
---
<Base title={title} description={description} path="/guides/mean-median-and-mode-explained">
  <aside class="ad-rail" aria-label="Advertisement"><div class="ad-slot ad-slot-rail"></div></aside>
  <div class="wrap article">
    <p class="note"><a href="/guides">Guides</a> / Statistics</p>
    <h1>Mean, Median and Mode: Which Average Should You Use?</h1>
    <p class="lead">"Average" usually means the mean, but the median and mode are also averages. Each one describes a list of numbers differently, and choosing the right one avoids a misleading picture.</p>
    <div class="ad-slot" aria-label="Advertisement"></div>
<h2>Mean</h2>
<p>Add all the values and divide by how many there are. The numbers 4, 8, 15, 15, 16, 23 and 42 add up to 123, and 123 ÷ 7 is about 17.57.</p>
<h2>Median</h2>
<p>Sort the numbers and take the middle one. With 7 values, the median is the fourth one, which is 15. With an even count, the median is the average of the two middle values.</p>
<h2>Mode</h2>
<p>The mode is the value that appears most often. In the same list, 15 appears twice and the other values once, so the mode is 15. A list where every value appears once has no mode.</p>
<h2>Why outliers matter</h2>
<p>Add one very large value, such as 400, and the mean jumps, while the median barely moves. That is why incomes and house prices are usually reported as medians.</p>
<h2>Try it yourself</h2>
<p>The <a href="/tools/mean-median-mode-calculator">Mean, Median and Mode Calculator</a> works out all three, plus the range and count, from any list you paste.</p>
      <section class="worked">
        <h2>Worked example</h2>
        <p>In the list 2, 3, 3, 4, 100: the mean is (2 + 3 + 3 + 4 + 100) ÷ 5 = 22.4, which is pulled up by the 100. The median is 3, the middle value. The mode is 3, because it appears twice. The median describes the typical value better here.</p>
      </section>
      <section class="faq-list">
        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <details><summary>When should I use the median?</summary><p>Use it when a few very large or small values would distort the mean, such as incomes or house prices.</p></details>
        <details><summary>What happens if every value appears once?</summary><p>There is no mode, because no value is more common than the others.</p></details>
        <details><summary>What is the median of an even number of values?</summary><p>Sort the values, then average the two middle ones. For 2, 4, 6, 8 the median is (4 + 6) ÷ 2 = 5.</p></details>
      </section>
